Title: Diagnosis

Details: The failing test method skipUsingRead() tests the skip method of IOUtils by wrapping an InputStream in a FilterInputStream whose skip(long s) method always returns 0. This simulates a stream where the skip method does not skip any bytes.

The relevant code snippet of IOUtils.skip(InputStream input, long numToSkip) reveals the cause of the failure and the underlying bug:

```java
public static long skip(InputStream input, long numToSkip) throws IOException {
    long available = numToSkip;
    while (numToSkip > 0) {
        long skipped = input.skip(numToSkip);
        if (skipped == 0) {
            break;
        }
        numToSkip -= skipped;
    }
    return available - numToSkip;
}
```

The method attempts to skip the requested number of bytes by calling skip on the InputStream in a loop. If skip returns 0 (meaning no bytes skipped), it breaks out of the loop and returns the number of bytes skipped.

The problem is that the method assumes that skip() will only return 0 when the end of stream (EOF) is reached. This assumption is explicitly mentioned in the bug report quoting the javadoc. However, some InputStream implementations do not adhere to this: for example, the FilterInputStream's anonymous subclass in the test always returns 0 from skip(long), simulating a stream that never skips bytes.

Under these circumstances, IOUtils.skip returns prematurely without continuing reading or doing anything to fulfill the requested skip amount. This violates the javadoc specification that it should skip as many bytes as possible until EOF.

Thus, the root cause is that IOUtils.skip relies solely on the underlying InputStream.skip behavior and does not fall back on reading and discarding bytes when skip returns 0 but EOF has not been reached.

This mismatch between the implementation and the documented contract leads to the failure of the test skipUsingRead and is the likely cause of the user's issue in the bug report that references feeding TarInputStream from CipherInputStream where skip is unreliable.

Recent bug reports do not add relevant insights for this IOUtils.skip specific issue.

Summary: IOUtils.skip() incorrectly relies on InputStream.skip to always work or reach end-of-stream when returning 0, whereas some InputStreams may return 0 without EOF, causing IOUtils.skip to stop skipping prematurely. This behavior contradicts its javadoc specification and leads to failures in skipping functionality.

